[Music]  guys today we are going to be learning  how to make  puff pastry now at the beginning of the  quarantine i started baking  and using store bought puff pastry like  beverage farms and stuff like that by  the middle of quarantine i realized  in order to step up my baking game i  needed to make  my own puff pastry i need to understand  the science behind puff pastry so i  enlisted the help of my friend julia she  came over one day and we  sat down and she taught me how to make  puff pastry from scratch now legend has  it that puff pastry is very hard to make  there's the folding of the butter into  the dough and then there's the  temperature part of it where you have to  keep the dough  and the butter at a certain temperature  so it doesn't melt but at the end of it  all i realize it's not that scary and  it's not that daunting to make  it's actually very very easy although  tedious it is super super manageable and  i'm going to teach you how to make puffs  pastry from scratch  today  [Music]  all right first let's go through the  ingredients we'll need two cups  minus two tablespoons of all-purpose  flour and yes that's a very specific  amount of flour we'll also need a  quarter teaspoon of salt  we'll need three quarter cups plus two  tablespoons  of cold water and finally we'll need one  and a half sticks of unsalted butter  room temperature let's move on into  making our dough  so first i like to sift the flour into a  large mixing bowl get out any large  clumps of flour  next add your salt and then add your  cold water now combine all the  ingredients together and work it into a  dough  now once you have your dough it should  be a sticky dough forming into a bowl  and because it's a sticky dough you want  to use a lot of flour to dust your work  surface and cover it with the mixing  bowl  and now we're going to let it rest for  15 minutes just so that it can get to  room temperature and during that time  we're going to head over to our butter  hopefully you've taken out the butter  like an hour beforehand so it can get  down to room temperature if not  throw it in the microwave and microwave  it for roughly i don't know like 20  seconds  15 seconds don't melt the butter you  just want it to be at room temperature  so whenever you do have room temperature  butter  go ahead and stir it and get it to be a  smooth  buttery paste  all right now we have our dough ball  ready to go at room temp we have the  butter  at room temp and it's all whipped up now  we are finally ready  for the folding process and here is fold  number one to begin we'll need a rolling  pin  a rubber scraper and a pastry knife like  always when baking flour your work area  generously and  gently press down on your dough and form  it into a mini  rectangle with your hands once it's  pushed down i find it easier to roll out  so next use your rolling pin  and roll out that dough into a 16 by 12  rectangle  now once you have it rolled out imagine  your dough  in sections of thirds seeing your dough  in thirds is going to be super key  so we're gonna take our butter we're  gonna apply it to two thirds of our  dough  surface so what i like to do is draw  imaginary line where we section off one  third of the dough and that one third  we're not gonna touch it with any butter  the remaining two thirds of it we're  gonna lather on the butter now be sure  to only use a quarter of your butter at  this point because we're going to be  repeating this process  four four times now once you have the  butter applied  go ahead and fold the unbuttered third  of the dough  over at the cut off line over into the  buttered section and now you're gonna  have  the remaining one-third of the buttered  section  exposed and we're gonna fold that over  to the part we just fold it in  so it's kind of like you're making one  of those uh travel brochure pamphlets  with  dough and i talk about all this folding  stuff for a reason because this folding  stuff is the very reason why there are  layers  in puff pastry and also the very reason  why we're going to put this whole thing  into the fridge so that the butter  doesn't actually melt into the flour  we want to distinguish that butter and  the flour  as separate entities so that they can  retain that layering effect  so from here i like to fold in the edges  and i'm going to take some plastic wrap  and wrap it in plastic wrap and we're  going to place it into the fridge for  15 minutes just enough for the butter to  harden up all right after 15 minutes  it is time for fold number two out comes  the dough  we're gonna put it back on a floured  surface and again  roll it out into a 16 by 12 rectangle  all right so again  repeating the last step in fact the next  two steps are also repeating  this step in the last step it's all the  same it's all ditto  so imagine in thirds again apply butter  on two thirds of your dough  fold the unbuttered third into the  middle of your dough  and fold the remaining butter dough on  top of the rest of it making that  pamphlet again  i guess there is one thing i do  differently at this step since there  already is  butter layered in i like to also fold  the pamphlet shape that it's in  into itself one more time just to create  some more layers since the butter is  already there  so i'm just constantly folding and  folding and folding and folding now  there is actually a formula out there  where you based on the number of times  you fold and the number of times you  repeat the process  you can actually calculate the number of  final layers you'll have  at the moment i don't want to do any  math so i'm not going to tell you what  that equation is  i'm sure you can wikipedia and find out  for yourself anyways back to our dough  once it's folded in like this  go ahead and place it back to the fridge  for another 15 minutes  all right round number three ditto  imagine in thirds we're going to lather  it up on two things fold it up to the  one third side and then  halfway and you fold it to the center  one more time yada yada and by this time  you'll actually start feeling the dough  feel actually buttery while you're  working on it your hands are warm and  you know  some of that butter will escape here and  there you'll actually feel that  butteriness  in your dough so as you feel that  butteriness in your hands it's a  constant reminder to work fast and get  that thing back into the fridge as soon  as you can so once you're done with fold  number three let's go ahead and place it  back into the fridge for another  15 minutes all right and round number  four we are now going to do it  one last time same exact thing are you  tired yet but you know what all this  hard work and all this highly  sophisticated engineering of butter  folded into dough  it's gonna pay off in the long run  you're gonna thank yourself once you  bite into that puff pastry  after we bake it so after foley is one  final time you are now officially  done give yourself a pound on the back  and this  is equal to 12 servings of egg tarts 12  servings of pastries  however you want it but that also  depends on how much pastry you use how i  use up this pastry it's about 12  servings for me and if you don't know  what to use your puff pastry for  if you're into something savory at the  moment i have a delicious curry puff  video  or you can check out my portuguese egg  tart video which is my fave  if you're into something sweet at the  moment anyways like always make sure to  like and subscribe feel free to leave a  comment down below if you have any ideas  for me to make next and i will see you  next time  [Music]  you

